{"reference":"1 Corinthians 7","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Marriage, singleness, and temporary abstinence","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter distinguishes several kinds of counsel: Paul calls some directions a concession or his judgment, while permitting both marriage and singleness. In 7:1-7, spouses are addressed reciprocally, and temporary abstinence is specifically to be by mutual agreement and followed by reunion. Later advice about the unmarried is shaped by a “present distress,” and the people called “virgins” may be understood in more than one way—such as virgins, betrothed women, guardians, or partners. These qualifications keep the passage from being read as a single, universal rule for every marriage decision.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 7:1-7","1 Corinthians 7:25-40","1 Corinthians 7:6-9"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_007_dec494a8efa79fe52bb0a9fa"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"In the counsel about mixed marriages, remaining together is conditioned on the unbelieving spouse being willing to live with the believer; if that spouse leaves, Paul permits the departure and invokes peace. Alongside the chapter’s esteem for both marriage and singleness, this means the passage should not be used to compel either status or to trap adults or children in domestic or sexual danger.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 7:10-16","1 Corinthians 7:25-40","1 Corinthians 7:6-9"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_007_7a0bc45229b23916e99b7c83"]}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Calling, status, and embodied identity","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s repeated instruction to remain in one’s calling addresses a community that included households, enslaved and free people, patrons, workers, and people with unequal resources and vulnerability. It is pastoral counsel within those conditions, not a universal ban on freedom, education, social mobility, or leaving harmful work. The chapter speaks within a slaveholding society without morally validating slavery; moreover, the phrase “use it rather” in verse 21 is grammatically disputed, so certainty about its precise advice concerning freedom should remain limited.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 7:17-24","1 Corinthians 7:21","1 Corinthians 7:21-24","1 Corinthians 7:25-38","1 Corinthians 7:26-31"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_007_11fab56c5b4eb20000173dfd","reader_path_1_corinthians_007_96be45447e28f579aa0014f8"]},{"id":"block_4","text":"Verses 17-20 tell circumcised people not to seek reversal and uncircumcised people not to seek circumcision, placing both statuses beneath keeping God’s commands. The discussion concerns ancient circumcision, reversal, rivalry, and embodied status; it does not directly address modern intersex surgery or clinical consent questions. It therefore should not be used to support compelled bodily alteration, bodily mockery, or forced assimilation.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 7:17-20","1 Corinthians 7:17-24"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_007_80ef2b9e05545096fa501745"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The letter’s transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"For First Corinthians as a whole, manuscripts such as P15, P46, P123, and several major codices preserve different portions and textual variants. No surviving witness simply equals the original manuscript. The extant letter is best read as a coherent rhetorical whole, while proposals about local additions or divisions of the letter remain matters to assess passage by passage.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_007_ed0665089ba02e2c52b6b303"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}